&lt;section id="others-to-explore" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Others &amp; To-explore&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;section id="marginal-inference-approach" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Marginal inference approach&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;We discussed instead of the joint-inference approach, we could sample &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho_{ij}\)&lt;/span&gt; independently from its asymptotic distribution &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(N(\rho_{ij}, 1-\rho_{ij}^2)\)&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;From simulation (similar to simulation 2 above), coverage of each &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho_{ij}\)&lt;/span&gt; is close to the nominal level when considered individually. However, when we consider the results across all &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho_{ij}\)&lt;/span&gt; s jointly, the joint coverage from this marginal approach became poor. This is related to multiple testing problem.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;To construct a correlation matrix sample, we could sample each &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho_{ij}\)&lt;/span&gt; independently from its confidence interval, and check if that constructed matrix is PSD. If it is not PSD, we reject it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This approach works in lower dimension, however became highly inefficient (large rejection rate) as dimension grows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="docs/plots/RejectionSamplingRate.png" class="img-fluid" width="399" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="eta-as-a-metric-to-to-capture-the-amount-of-faithfulness" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\eta\)&lt;/span&gt; as a metric to to capture the amount of faithfulness?&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;How can we estimate &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\eta\)&lt;/span&gt; ? Methods of moments should work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Use case: given an observed correlation matrix, estimate &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\eta\)&lt;/span&gt;, we use this as a proxy to gauge whether our pertubation approach would work.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Caveat: we only have one observation (sample correlation), how can &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\eta\)&lt;/span&gt; still be estimated meaningfully?&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="kl-divergence" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;KL Divergence&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Expectation of the logarithmic difference between two distribution P and Q. Further explore if KL divergence can be a stronger statistical distance than mahalanobis distance.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="sampling-idea-bryan" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Sampling Idea (Bryan)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Construct a list of Rs that only contains Rhat (the sample correlation) to begin with&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Uniformly pick a correlation matrix from your list of Rs (call it A)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sample a new correlation matrix (call it B) using your sampling distribution (95% confidence region using wald distance) with A as the input correlation matrix&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Check if Rhat is within Bs 95% confidence region and add B to your list of Rs if it is, otherwise do nothing&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Go to step 2 (until Rs has reached a desired size)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="distributions" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Distributions:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;If both true mean and covariance are unknown, and we used both sample mean and sample covariance to estimate / compute mahalanobis distance, the resulting distances could potentially follow something more conservative than chi-square, perhaps F/ Hotelling T2.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="some-other-things" class="level3"&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Some other things&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Perturbation radius versus SHD&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Distribution of mahalanobis distance (see sim 4-2)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Inspect BOSS, performance should not be that bad (tbc).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Idea: if a correlation matrix is ill-conditioned (eg. highly colinear), is it better to perturb using a subset of those (i.e., select those stable dimensions/ variables and run perturbations). - can be a future direction.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="references" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;References&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;ol type="1"&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kalisch, M., Mächler, M., Colombo, D., Maathuis, M. H., &amp; Bühlmann, P. (2012). Causal Inference Using Graphical Models with the &lt;em&gt;R&lt;/em&gt; Package &lt;strong&gt;pcalg&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;Journal of Statistical Software&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;47&lt;/em&gt;(11). &lt;a href="https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v047.i11" class="uri"&gt;https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v047.i11&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;Kummerfeld, E., Williams, L., &amp; Ma, S. (2024). Power analysis for causal discovery. &lt;em&gt;International Journal of Data Science and Analytics&lt;/em&gt;, &lt;em&gt;17&lt;/em&gt;(3), 289–304. &lt;a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s41060-023-00399-4" class="uri"&gt;https://doi.org/10.1007/s41060-023-00399-4&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Steiger, J. H. (1990).&lt;/strong&gt; Structural model evaluation and modification: An interval estimation approach. &lt;em&gt;Multivariate Behavioral Research, 25&lt;/em&gt;(2), 173-180.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/section&gt;
&lt;section id="notations-depreciated" class="level1"&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;Notations (depreciated)&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R\)&lt;/span&gt;: True R, unknown&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R_{obs}\)&lt;/span&gt;: Observed R, typically estimate from sample data&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R^{\prime}_{obs}\)&lt;/span&gt;: Use superscript “&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\prime\)&lt;/span&gt;” to denote a perturbed object&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho\)&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(vech(R)\)&lt;/span&gt;, the unique off-diagonal elements of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R\)&lt;/span&gt; matrix.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho\)&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(vech(R_{obs})\)&lt;/span&gt;, the unique off-diagonal elements of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R_{obs}\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\hat\rho^{\prime}\)&lt;/span&gt;: &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(vech(R^{\prime}_{obs})\)&lt;/span&gt;, the unique off-diagonal elements of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(R^{\prime}_{obs}\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\mu\)&lt;/span&gt;: population mean&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\Sigma\)&lt;/span&gt;: Covariance matrix&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\Psi\)&lt;/span&gt;: Covariance matrix of pearson correlation coefficient&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(d\)&lt;/span&gt;: number of variables in data&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(p\)&lt;/span&gt;: number of parameters (length of &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\rho\)&lt;/span&gt;) = &lt;span class="math inline"&gt;\(\frac{d(d-1)}{2}\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
